    "narrative": "The Austin Waves enter today\u2019s slate with **Luis Garc\u00eda Jr.** serving as our clear focal point, boasting the highest efficiency score at 74.38. His matchup against Braydon Fisher is highly favorable, as the combination of a 4.37 xERA and a potent dynamic platoon advantage (+14%) allows him to project as our premier offensive engine. **Drake Baldwin** also warrants high confidence at 70.83; he benefits significantly from Eduardo Rodriguez\u2019s 4.71 xERA and a top-of-the-order boost that maximizes his opportunities to capitalize on a soft matchup. While **Jacob Wilson** faces a tougher test against MacKenzie Gore, his 63.95 score remains robust, bolstered by a 10% park factor bump that suggests he will handle the lefty-lefty dynamic effectively.\n\nNavigating the remainder of the lineup required careful calibration against elite arms. We are cautiously deploying **Andy Pages** and **Vladimir Guerrero Jr.** despite their encounters with Jacob Misiorowski and Cam Schlittler, respectively. Both stars see their base projections suppressed by the 2.03 and 2.81 xERAs of their opposing pitchers, though winds blowing out provide a critical floor-raising hedge for **Andy Pages**. Conversely, **Gunnar Henderson** represents our most painful bench decision. Despite his elite pedigree, the combination of a 3.34 xERA from Ian Seymour and a negative 15% platoon adjustment pushed his score to 43.19, forcing him out of the starting nine in favor of higher-efficiency matchups like **Heliot Ramos**, whose favorable 4.37 xERA matchup and stolen base upside against Fisher align better with our league-wide scarcity strategy.\n\nWe remain on high alert regarding the \"Lineup Pending\" status across the board, which introduces inherent volatility to our projected batting order assumptions. **Kyle Tucker** is active but carries risk; his efficiency score of 54.04 reflects a significant 19% downgrade due to the elite nature of Misiorowski, and we will monitor pre-game reports to ensure he is fully rested for the full nine innings. With the SB category acting as our primary lever, players like **Wade Meckler** (59.97) have been prioritized over high-power, low-speed options to ensure we remain competitive in our most scarce metric despite facing a volatile opponent in Randy Dobnak.",
